{"title":"Unit (ring theory)","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ring_theory\" title=\"Ring theory\">ring theory</a> we say that an element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ring\" title=\"Ring\">ring</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is a <b>unit</b> if it has an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inverse\" title=\"Inverse\">inverse</a> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, that is, if there is another element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/d/9bd33326ca5b0e1fc351efeffdbc3f18e9982ee8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v\\in R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/f/4ef46dc13f11d52463c81219b78d4af580a05936.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$uv=vu=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"96\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>It is easy to show that the set of units <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/2/f32154a8ac5a615230902b38f5e34586639283cd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U(R)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> of a ring forms a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Group\" title=\"Group\">group</a> under multiplication. This group is known as the <b>group of units</b> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Unit_(ring_theory)&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018112930\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.010 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.014 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 28/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 295/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1210/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.971      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.971      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:8369:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018112930 and revision id 32732. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}